{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit and accrued interest on transfer of rental property","requirement":"When rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property that is sold or transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for withholding security deposit of public assistance recipients","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit of a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who have received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected and enforced by summary proceedings under the Penalty Enforcement Law; the State entity that made the deposits is entitled to any penalty recovered.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawful use of security deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be criminally charged as a disorderly person and fined at least $200, imprisoned up to 30 days, or both.","key_value":"$200 minimum fine or up to 30 days' imprisonment","coverage_conditions":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Charged as a disorderly person; fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"may be subject to a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and no mid-lease increase","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent during a lease term; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease and offer the tenant a new lease at the increased rent with proper written notice, and the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; increases must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"The increase must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase bar after condominium or cooperative conversion","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, regardless of protected-tenancy eligibility.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings, for 30 years following completion of construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings exempt from local rent control for 30 years after construction"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause; most eviction causes must be stated in a written notice to quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence; written and oral leases; a Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-lawful-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting a housing unit because of a person's source of lawful income.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex residences.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exempt: two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, including YMCA and age-restricted housing as to familial status.","penalty":"Fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
